 ],
 "literal": "If my friend gave the green unripe fruit of the neem tree, / 'Sweet flower-like sugar candy!' you said. But now, / even if she gives the cool, clear water of the cold mountain pool of Pari's Parampu hill, / in the cold month of Thai, / 'It is hot and salty!' you say. / O Lord, such is indeed the nature of your love!",
 "translation": "When my friend gave you the bitter green fruit of the neem tree, you said, 'It is sweet sugar candy!' But now, even if she offers you the clear, cool water from the mountain pool of Pari's Parampu hill in the chilly month of Thai, you say, 'It is hot and brackish!' O Lord, such is the nature of your love!",
 "modern": "என் தோழி வேப்ப மரத்தின் பச்சைக் காயைக் கொடுத்தபோது, 'இது இனிப்பான சர்க்கரைக் கட்டி!' என்று கூறினீர். ஆனால் இப்பொழுது, பாரியினுடைய பறம்பு மலையில் உள்ள குளிர்ந்த சுனையின் தெளிந்த நீரை, தை மாதத்துக் குளிர் காலத்தில் அவள் கொண்டு வந்து கொடுத்தாலும், 'இது சூடாகவும் உவர்ப்பாகவும் இருக்கிறது!' என்று கூறுகிறீர். ஐயனே! உமது அன்பின் தன்மை இப்படிப்பட்டது போலும்!",
 "explanation": "The friend of the heroine is speaking to the hero who has returned from his infidelity. She sarcastically points out how his perception of the heroine has completely changed over time. In the early days of their courtship, even the bitterest neem fruit given by her tasted like sweet sugar candy to him. Now, when his love has faded, even the sweetest, coolest water from the famous Parampu hill of chieftain Pari during the coldest month of Thai tastes hot and salty to him. This contrast beautifully illustrates the subjective nature of love and belongs to the Marutam landscape, which deals with infidelity and sulking. The key words are 'வேம்பு' (neem), 'தேம்பூங்கட்டி' (sweet sugar candy), and 'அன்பின் பால்' (the nature of love).",
